1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,236
April twenty-fourth, nineteen sixty-four.

2
00:00:03,236 --> 00:00:13,505
A police officer in a small New Mexico town drives over a hill and sees a shiny white egg on legs in a gully, and two figures in white beside it.

3
00:00:13,505 --> 00:00:16,833
Minutes later, the bushes are still smoldering.

4
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,956
The Air Force looked into it and filed it as unknown.

5
00:00:20,956 --> 00:00:22,543
The FBI looked into it.

6
00:00:22,543 --> 00:00:27,925
A famous scientist who came to doubt it left convinced the witness was honest.

7
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:33,100
And yet, sixty years later, a handwritten note says it was a student prank.

8
00:00:33,566 --> 00:00:37,882
Tonight we measure the case the way he lived it, in distance.

9
00:00:37,882 --> 00:00:38,518
One mile.

10
00:00:38,518 --> 00:00:39,862
Eight hundred feet.

11
00:00:39,862 --> 00:00:40,924
A hundred feet.

12
00:00:40,924 --> 00:00:41,277
Zero.

13
00:00:41,277 --> 00:00:46,866
At each range we ask what he saw, what can be proved, and what is only a story.

14
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:48,745
The Socorro landing.

15
00:00:48,745 --> 00:00:50,439
Evidence against theory.

16
00:00:50,439 --> 00:00:52,133
Let us start a mile out.

17
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:57,570
One mile out.

18
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:00,554
Socorro, New Mexico, a Friday afternoon.

19
00:01:00,554 --> 00:01:09,733
Fewer than ten thousand people live here, along the Rio Grande, beside a small college that teaches mining and engineering.

20
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,502
Patrolman Lonnie Zamora is local.

21
00:01:12,502 --> 00:01:16,828
Before he joined the police, he worked on that college campus.

22
00:01:16,828 --> 00:01:21,224
Colleagues call him sober, steady, a man who follows the rules.

23
00:01:21,224 --> 00:01:23,666
Tonight, that will count for a lot.

24
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:34,599
At about a quarter to six, he is chasing a speeding car south of town when he hears a roar, and sees a bluish-orange flame sinking in the sky to the southwest.

25
00:01:35,066 --> 00:01:39,523
There is a dynamite shack out that way, used by local miners.

26
00:01:39,523 --> 00:01:41,715
Zamora thinks it has exploded.

27
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:46,099
He drops the chase, and turns up a rough gravel road to see.

28
00:01:46,566 --> 00:01:49,509
The hill is steep and the gravel is loose.

29
00:01:49,509 --> 00:01:53,574
It takes him three tries to get the patrol car to the top.

30
00:01:53,574 --> 00:01:54,695
Hold on to that.

31
00:01:54,695 --> 00:01:58,899
It is a mundane detail, and it will matter for the timeline.

32
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:02,071
At a mile, there is only Zamora's word.

33
00:02:02,071 --> 00:02:02,904
He is alone.

34
00:02:02,904 --> 00:02:04,776
No one else sees the flame.

35
00:02:04,776 --> 00:02:13,099
A few tourists would later say they saw something low in the sky, but those stories are patchy, and they came afterward.

36
00:02:13,566 --> 00:02:14,858
Eight hundred feet.

37
00:02:14,858 --> 00:02:18,392
He reaches the crest, and looks down into an arroyo.

38
00:02:18,392 --> 00:02:20,500
Something shiny is lying there.

39
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:24,094
His first thought is a car turned on its roof.

40
00:02:24,094 --> 00:02:28,037
It is whitish, he will say, like aluminum, but not chrome.

41
00:02:28,037 --> 00:02:34,700
He estimates it is a hundred and fifty to two hundred yards away, and twelve to fifteen feet tall.

42
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:37,293
Then it looks wrong for a car.

43
00:02:37,293 --> 00:02:40,057
It is oval, like an egg, or a football.

44
00:02:40,057 --> 00:02:45,800
It seems to stand on two legs that slant outward, and it has no windows or doors.

45
00:02:46,266 --> 00:02:49,660
And beside it, two figures in white coveralls.

46
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:53,274
Small, he says, maybe small adults or large kids.

47
00:02:53,274 --> 00:02:57,110
One turns toward his car, seems startled, and jumps.

48
00:02:57,110 --> 00:02:59,766
All of this lasts about two seconds.

49
00:03:00,233 --> 00:03:08,137
He loses sight of it, drives on a little, and radios the dispatcher, Nep Lopez, that he is checking a possible wreck.

50
00:03:08,137 --> 00:03:12,933
Then he asks Lopez to look out the window and see if anything is there.

51
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:18,425
When Lopez asks what it looks like, Zamora answers with five words.

52
00:03:18,425 --> 00:03:20,224
It looks like a balloon.

53
00:03:20,224 --> 00:03:21,574
Notice the choice.

54
00:03:21,574 --> 00:03:22,324
A balloon.

55
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:23,150
Not a ship.

56
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:24,199
Not a monster.

57
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:27,703
Two days later he sits for a radio interview.

58
00:03:27,703 --> 00:03:31,078
The reporter asks whether the figures were people.

59
00:03:31,078 --> 00:03:33,913
Zamora says no sir, he would not say that.

60
00:03:33,913 --> 00:03:38,233
He saw something white, white coveralls, that is all he can say.

61
00:03:38,699 --> 00:03:39,593
Remember that.

62
00:03:39,593 --> 00:03:43,996
The man at the center of this story never claimed to meet a spaceman.

63
00:03:43,996 --> 00:03:46,166
Later retellings did that for him.

64
00:03:46,633 --> 00:03:47,595
A hundred feet.

65
00:03:47,595 --> 00:03:53,433
He stops the car on the ridge, about a hundred and three feet from the gully, and gets out.

66
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:58,239
He hears two or three loud thumps, like someone slamming a car door.

67
00:03:58,239 --> 00:04:00,599
He walks a few steps toward the edge.

68
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:03,553
Then a roar begins, low and rising.

69
00:04:03,553 --> 00:04:08,172
A flame appears under the object, blue and orange, with no smoke.

70
00:04:08,172 --> 00:04:10,233
And the thing starts to lift.

71
00:04:10,699 --> 00:04:14,350
On its side, he sees a red mark about two feet high.

72
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:16,736
He will draw it for investigators.

73
00:04:16,736 --> 00:04:19,614
An inverted V, with three lines under it.

74
00:04:19,614 --> 00:04:23,966
It looks less like a star symbol and more like a company logo.

75
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:26,692
He thinks it is about to explode.

76
00:04:26,692 --> 00:04:34,633
He runs, puts the car between himself and the craft, loses his sunglasses, and throws himself down behind the crest.

77
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,534
Then the roar stops.

78
00:04:36,534 --> 00:04:38,542
He hears a short high whine.

79
00:04:38,542 --> 00:04:44,566
He looks up, and the object is moving away, low and very fast, with no flame at all.

80
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:48,528
It clears the dynamite shack by about three feet.

81
00:04:48,528 --> 00:04:54,092
It climbs slowly over the desert, and shrinks toward mountains six miles away.

82
00:04:54,092 --> 00:04:55,233
Then it is gone.

83
00:04:55,699 --> 00:04:57,315
He races to the radio.

84
00:04:57,315 --> 00:05:07,300
By the time Sergeant Sam Chavez of the State Police arrives, he finds a man who is visibly shaken, and a hillside that is still smoking.

85
00:05:07,766 --> 00:05:08,515
Zero feet.

86
00:05:08,515 --> 00:05:11,811
Chavez and Zamora walk down into the arroyo.

87
00:05:11,811 --> 00:05:14,132
Greasewood bushes are scorched.

88
00:05:14,132 --> 00:05:19,899
Undersheriff Jim Luckie arrives and says the brush is still hot to the touch.

89
00:05:20,366 --> 00:05:24,352
In the ground, four rectangular dents in a rough square.

90
00:05:24,352 --> 00:05:32,466
The FBI will measure them at about sixteen inches long, six wide, and two deep, each pushing dirt to the far side.

91
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:35,854
Inside the dents, burned clumps of grass.

92
00:05:35,854 --> 00:05:38,774
A further burned patch lies outside them.

93
00:05:38,774 --> 00:05:41,766
And there are three circular marks nearby.

94
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:48,047
An FBI agent named Arthur Byrnes happens to be in Socorro on other business.

95
00:05:48,047 --> 00:05:52,560
He drives to the site that evening and measures everything.

96
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:58,833
His memo calls Zamora sober, industrious, conscientious, and not given to fantasy.

97
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:07,311
The next morning, the local newspaper editor photographs the holes and the burned bushes, before crowds trample them.

98
00:06:07,311 --> 00:06:09,228
So the marks are documented.

99
00:06:09,228 --> 00:06:11,282
No one photographs the object.

100
00:06:11,282 --> 00:06:13,199
It left before anyone could.

101
00:06:13,666 --> 00:06:15,641
And then the crowds arrive.

102
00:06:15,641 --> 00:06:19,811
Within days, cars line the road and the site is trampled.

103
00:06:19,811 --> 00:06:21,860
When Air Force consultant J.

104
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:27,566
Allen Hynek arrives, he finds that little of scientific value can be obtained.

105
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:33,455
Hynek is an astronomer who has spent years explaining UFO reports away.

106
00:06:33,455 --> 00:06:37,656
He interviews Zamora, and comes away sure of one thing.

107
00:06:37,656 --> 00:06:41,933
This man is not lying, and this is not a hoax by Zamora.

108
00:06:42,399 --> 00:06:44,227
The Air Force goes wide.

109
00:06:44,227 --> 00:06:46,969
A Geiger counter finds no radiation.

110
00:06:46,969 --> 00:06:53,289
Soil and burned brush are analyzed, with no foreign material and no rocket residue.

111
00:06:53,289 --> 00:06:56,792
Radar at Holloman and Albuquerque saw nothing.

112
00:06:56,792 --> 00:07:00,600
Balloon, helicopter and aircraft records, nothing.

113
00:07:01,066 --> 00:07:08,242
They even write to companies building lunar vehicles, asking if any test craft was loose in New Mexico.

114
00:07:08,242 --> 00:07:09,566
Every answer is no.

115
00:07:10,033 --> 00:07:14,383
Blue Book director Hector Quintanilla sums it up in one line.

116
00:07:14,383 --> 00:07:19,947
About Zamora, he writes, he is puzzled by what he saw, and frankly, so are we.

117
00:07:19,947 --> 00:07:22,800
The case goes into the files as unknown.

118
00:07:23,266 --> 00:07:25,210
Here is what the ground proves.

119
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:31,984
Something burned those bushes and pressed those four marks, within minutes, while a trained officer watched.

120
00:07:31,984 --> 00:07:33,928
Here is what it does not prove.

121
00:07:33,928 --> 00:07:35,433
What that something was.

122
00:07:35,899 --> 00:07:36,664
Wide angle.

123
00:07:36,664 --> 00:07:41,458
When a case sits in the files as unknown, five suspects come forward.

124
00:07:41,458 --> 00:07:46,599
Let us line up the conventional ones first, and then the one you came for.

125
00:07:47,066 --> 00:07:49,162
Suspect one: a lunar lander.

126
00:07:49,162 --> 00:08:00,162
In nineteen sixty-four, NASA was testing a model of Surveyor, the moon probe, in this part of New Mexico, and a helicopter carried it the same day.

127
00:08:00,162 --> 00:08:02,033
Some writers saw a match.

128
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:03,682
It does not fit.

129
00:08:03,682 --> 00:08:08,708
The test range is about a hundred and fifty kilometers from Socorro.

130
00:08:08,708 --> 00:08:15,138
Surveyor was a tripod of aluminum trusses, not an egg, and was never built to take off.

131
00:08:15,138 --> 00:08:17,872
Blue Book also asked the lunar firms.

132
00:08:17,872 --> 00:08:18,833
They said no.

133
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:20,913
Suspect two: a mirage.

134
00:08:20,913 --> 00:08:28,103
One skeptic suggested that Zamora and his fellow witnesses saw the star Canopus warped by the air.

135
00:08:28,103 --> 00:08:32,578
At a quarter to six on an April afternoon, in broad daylight.

136
00:08:32,578 --> 00:08:36,466
And a mirage cannot press four dents into the ground.

137
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:39,173
Suspect three: a publicity stunt.

138
00:08:39,173 --> 00:08:46,708
In nineteen sixty-eight, the skeptic Philip Klass argued that Socorro invented the landing to attract tourists.

139
00:08:46,708 --> 00:08:53,700
But a local historian checked one of his claims, that the mayor owned the land, and found it was wrong.

140
00:08:54,166 --> 00:08:56,203
Suspect four is more serious.

141
00:08:56,203 --> 00:09:07,157
In nineteen sixty-eight, the chemist Linus Pauling wrote to Stirling Colgate, president of New Mexico Tech, and asked what the college thought about Zamora.

142
00:09:07,157 --> 00:09:09,966
Colgate scribbled a reply at the bottom.

143
00:09:10,433 --> 00:09:15,177
I have good indication of a student who engineered the hoax, he wrote.

144
00:09:15,177 --> 00:09:16,600
The student has left.

145
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:21,480
Decades later, he told a researcher the craft was a candle in a balloon.

146
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:22,700
Not sophisticated.

147
00:09:23,166 --> 00:09:24,762
The details seem to fit.

148
00:09:24,762 --> 00:09:34,473
The college stocked fireworks and chemicals, lab coats were everywhere, and Zamora, who had worked on campus, was said to be strict with students.

149
00:09:34,473 --> 00:09:37,399
And his first word on the radio was balloon.

150
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:39,286
But look at the gaps.

151
00:09:39,286 --> 00:09:41,382
No student has ever been named.

152
00:09:41,382 --> 00:09:43,073
No method has been shown.

153
00:09:43,073 --> 00:09:52,066
A prank would have to burn bushes, dent hard ground, make a thunderous roar, and vanish before Sergeant Chavez arrived minutes later.

154
00:09:52,533 --> 00:09:59,418
Even the skeptic who revived the theory, Robert Sheaffer, admits it is hard to see how they got away.

155
00:09:59,418 --> 00:10:06,166
It is the most complete ordinary explanation on the table, and it is still a theory without a name.

156
00:10:06,633 --> 00:10:10,989
Suspect five, the one you came for: a visitor from another world.

157
00:10:10,989 --> 00:10:14,542
Egg-shaped ship, landing legs, a flame, a quick exit.

158
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:19,166
If it was real, it explains every strange thing in the story at once.

159
00:10:19,633 --> 00:10:21,213
But look at the details.

160
00:10:21,213 --> 00:10:23,518
The occupants wore white coveralls.

161
00:10:23,518 --> 00:10:27,404
The ship carried a red mark that looks like a company logo.

162
00:10:27,404 --> 00:10:33,200
If those are visitors, they dress like human technicians, and brand their ships like us.

163
00:10:33,666 --> 00:10:36,791
No other witness could be found by Blue Book.

164
00:10:36,791 --> 00:10:40,472
A local paper mentions a tourist who saw a low craft.

165
00:10:40,472 --> 00:10:42,069
The city now says five.

166
00:10:42,069 --> 00:10:47,833
Those stories came years later, and they are the kind that grow with every telling.

167
00:10:48,299 --> 00:10:51,065
So the alien theory needs one more thing.

168
00:10:51,065 --> 00:10:55,720
It needs a single piece of evidence that only an alien could produce.

169
00:10:55,720 --> 00:11:01,589
After sixty years, there is the sincerity of the witness, and four dents in the ground.

170
00:11:01,589 --> 00:11:02,466
Nothing else.

171
00:11:02,933 --> 00:11:04,256
Sixty years later.

172
00:11:04,256 --> 00:11:06,093
What happened to the man?

173
00:11:06,093 --> 00:11:07,930
Zamora stayed in Socorro.

174
00:11:07,930 --> 00:11:13,736
He served as a police officer for eight more years, then ran the town landfill.

175
00:11:13,736 --> 00:11:15,426
He hated the attention.

176
00:11:15,426 --> 00:11:18,733
He died in two thousand nine, at seventy-six.

177
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:21,078
He never changed his account.

178
00:11:21,078 --> 00:11:22,633
He never sold the story.

179
00:11:22,633 --> 00:11:25,807
He simply did not want to talk about it any more.

180
00:11:25,807 --> 00:11:30,666
Not one person who knew him has ever doubted that he believed what he said.

181
00:11:31,133 --> 00:11:32,793
The town did embrace it.

182
00:11:32,793 --> 00:11:39,432
In nineteen sixty-six, the Chamber of Commerce built stone walkways and benches around the spot.

183
00:11:39,432 --> 00:11:45,933
They put them a quarter mile from the real site, because of rumors the ground was radioactive.

184
00:11:46,399 --> 00:11:49,666
There is a mural, a festival and a tourist trail.

185
00:11:49,666 --> 00:11:52,533
A legend, in other words, with a gift shop.

186
00:11:52,533 --> 00:11:54,600
The legend is not the evidence.

187
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:55,799
It grew around it.

188
00:11:56,266 --> 00:11:57,617
Here is the lesson.

189
00:11:57,617 --> 00:12:00,888
There are two kinds of evidence in this story.

190
00:12:00,888 --> 00:12:07,502
The first is testimony: a sincere, consistent witness, believed by the FBI and the Air Force.

191
00:12:07,502 --> 00:12:11,200
The second is physical: burned brush and four dents.

192
00:12:11,666 --> 00:12:13,788
Neither identifies the thing.

193
00:12:13,788 --> 00:12:16,715
A truthful man can be fooled by a prank.

194
00:12:16,715 --> 00:12:20,227
A burned bush can be lit by anything that burns.

195
00:12:20,227 --> 00:12:23,666
Sincerity measures the witness, not the object.

196
00:12:24,133 --> 00:12:27,036
And the label unknown does not mean alien.

197
00:12:27,036 --> 00:12:32,083
It means the Air Force could not put a name on it in nineteen sixty-four.

198
00:12:32,083 --> 00:12:36,299
That is a statement about their paperwork, not about the sky.

199
00:12:36,766 --> 00:12:38,243
What could settle it?

200
00:12:38,243 --> 00:12:42,253
A named hoaxer, a confession, a photograph, a lab result.

201
00:12:42,253 --> 00:12:45,349
Sixty years of searching have produced none.

202
00:12:45,349 --> 00:12:48,866
The case sits exactly where the Air Force left it.

203
00:12:49,333 --> 00:12:50,819
Time to score the case.

204
00:12:50,819 --> 00:12:53,533
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

205
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,114
A sincere, consistent officer, backed by the FBI and Hynek: evidence.

206
00:12:59,114 --> 00:13:04,969
Four dents and burned brush, seen within minutes by several officers: evidence.

207
00:13:04,969 --> 00:13:09,565
A long list of Air Force checks that found no match: evidence.

208
00:13:09,565 --> 00:13:12,233
And what the object was: unverified.

209
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:14,761
Alien occupants: no evidence.

210
00:13:14,761 --> 00:13:16,681
The lunar lander: debunked.

211
00:13:16,681 --> 00:13:19,738
The student prank: plausible, never proved.

212
00:13:19,738 --> 00:13:23,933
Which leaves the honest answer on the board in plain sight.

213
00:13:24,399 --> 00:13:25,230
The verdict.

214
00:13:25,230 --> 00:13:32,014
A real event, in the sense that an honest officer saw something, and the ground carries the marks.

215
00:13:32,014 --> 00:13:36,028
A mystery, in the sense that no one has named what it was.

216
00:13:36,028 --> 00:13:37,067
But not aliens.

217
00:13:37,067 --> 00:13:39,766
Nothing in the file points to visitors.

218
00:13:40,233 --> 00:13:45,218
If you want the likeliest answer, it is a staged event that got out of hand.

219
00:13:45,218 --> 00:13:48,957
If you want the honest answer, it is that we do not know.

220
00:13:48,957 --> 00:13:52,500
Both are better than the story that grew up around it.

221
00:13:52,966 --> 00:13:55,860
Next case: Melbourne, nineteen sixty-six.

222
00:13:55,860 --> 00:14:02,354
Two hundred students and teachers watch a silver disc land in a paddock beside their school.

223
00:14:02,354 --> 00:14:06,166
Then the strangest part begins, and it is not the sky.

224
00:14:06,633 --> 00:14:09,267
Honest witness, hoax, or something else?

225
00:14:09,267 --> 00:14:14,799
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
